  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Pseudofistulina radicata is a wood-decay fungus in the Fistulinaceae family, characterized by its distinctive rooting base that extends into the substrate. This polypore produces a tough, bracket-like fruiting body with a unique appearance that sets it apart from other shelf fungi in New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Rooting Base",
      "description": "The most distinctive feature is the root-like or stalk-like attachment that extends downward into wood, unlike typical shelf fungi that attach flush to the substrate",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pore Surface",
      "description": "The underside displays small, angular pores rather than gills, and typically ranges from cream to pale yellow or tan in color",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Texture and Consistency",
      "description": "The fruiting body has a tough, woody texture that becomes harder with age and does not crumble easily when dry",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Pseudofistulina radicata grows on dead or dying hardwoods and logs in forests throughout New England, particularly on oak and other decaying wood in moist woodland settings.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The genus name 'Pseudofistulina' literally means 'false Fistulina,' as this fungus resembles the beefsteak fungus but lacks the radial gill-like structures that define true Fistulina species.",
